module System/Concurrency

import BuilderQuery
# TaskSpawnError / TaskTimeoutError are defined in System/Common.rf (alongside the other
# System crashables) so their synthesized represent/diagnose resolve crash_message correctly.

### Where a `Work[T]` runs, chosen by the verb that starts it. One concrete handle backs every place so a mixed
### set can be raced from a single `List[Work[T]]`.
choice WorkPlace
    ### Not started yet: `f.to[Work](...)` made it and no verb has run it.
    WAITING
    ### `start_now()`: a fiber on the runtime's pool of threads (M:N).
    NOW
    ### `start_here()`: a fiber on this thread's own scheduler (M:1).
    HERE
    ### `start_apart()`: an OS thread of its own (1:1).
    APART
    ### Consumed: a verb waited for it, took its value and freed what ran it.
    DONE

### A handle to work whose result you fetch with `retrieve()`. `f.to[Work](...)` makes one: it computes the arguments
### now and defers the call. A verb chooses where it runs and starts it:
###   * `start_now()` runs it on the runtime's pool of threads, the default for most work.
###   * `start_here()` runs it on this thread beside its other `start_here()` jobs, which take turns only where one
###     waits.
###   * `start_apart()` runs it on an OS thread of its own, for work that blocks or computes for long.
### `retrieve()` waits for the result (a Work no verb started is started with `start_now()` first), and
### `gather()` / `race()` do the same over a `List[Work[T]]`.
###
### RECIPE model: a Work is a recipe, the routine with its arguments bound. `f.to[Work](...)` builds the recipe (the
### arguments boxed into a heap recipe) and starts NOTHING. The verb makes the task block that will hold the
### result and the fiber or thread that runs `entry(recipe)`.
###
### A Work is an entity, so it has one original: `steal` hands it over (into a list, out of a routine), and a list
### element or a `waitfor` hands out access to it in place. When what holds it drops it (`drop job`, or the end
### of the scope that holds it) without a verb having taken its value, it is cancelled and waited for, so no work outlives the
### scope that started it. Work nothing started never runs.
###
### The verb that takes the value (`retrieve!`/`race!`/`gather!`) reaps what ran (fiber deleted or abandoned,
### result block freed) and marks the Work DONE. A recipe no verb ever runs leaks its recipe, and the routine body
### never runs.
entity Work[T]
    ### The work: where it runs, its fiber or thread, and its result.
    secret state: WorkState[T]
    ### The wait deadline of the next `retrieve!`; only meaningful when `has_deadline` is true (APART).
    secret deadline: Duration
    ### Whether `waitfor` gave the next `retrieve!` a deadline.
    secret has_deadline: Bool

### The state under a Work: a value that names one native state cell, so the language's own Work decides when it
### ends (`dispose`). A copy names the same cell. One more Work over the same work takes a hold
### with `add_holder`, and the work ends when the last holder disposes of it.
record WorkState[T]
    ### The state cell: slot 0 the place (0 WAITING, 1 NOW, 2 HERE, 3 APART, 4 DONE), slot 1 the fiber
    ### (rf_coro*) for NOW and HERE, slot 2 the task control block that holds the produced value, slot 3 nonzero
    ### when cancellation was requested before a verb started the work, slot 4 how many Work handles hold it, slot 5
    ### nonzero while the work is started but held back, waiting to be let go with the rest of its list.
    secret cell: Addr
    ### The routine the fiber or thread runs, given the recipe.
    secret entry: Addr
    ### The recipe: slot 0 holds the task, slot k the boxed k-th argument. The run routine reads the
    ### arguments out and frees it.
    secret recipe: Addr

### The size of one recipe slot (an `Addr`).
### :returns: the slot size.
routine recipe_slot_size() -> ByteSize
    return Addr.data_size()

### Allocates a recipe with `slots` Addr slots (slot 0 is the task, the rest are the arguments).
### Builder plumbing for `to[Work]` conversions.
### :returns: the recipe's address.
dangerous routine recipe_new(slots: U64) -> Addr
    return rf_allocate_dynamic(size: recipe_slot_size() * slots)

### Moves `value` into its own heap cell and stores the cell in recipe slot `slot` (like
### `List.add_last` storing an element). Builder plumbing for `to[Work]` conversions.
dangerous routine recipe_put[T](recipe: Addr, slot: U64, value: T)
    var cell = Hijacked[T](rf_allocate_dynamic(size: T.data_size()))
    cell.poke(value)
    Hijacked[Addr](recipe).poke_at_offset(at: recipe_slot_size() * slot, value: cell.address())
    return

### Moves the value out of recipe slot `slot` and frees its cell (like `List.remove_last`). Builder
### plumbing for the routine that runs a recipe.
### :returns: the argument value.
dangerous routine recipe_take[T](recipe: Addr, slot: U64) -> T
    var cell = Hijacked[Addr](recipe).peek_at_offset(offset: recipe_slot_size() * slot)
    var value = Hijacked[T](cell).peek()
    C::rf_invalidate(ptr: cell)
    return value

### Runs `entry(recipe)` for a call of a foreign routine marked `@blocking`: inside a coroutine on one of the
### runtime's I/O threads while the coroutine parks, so its worker goes on with other coroutines, anywhere else
### right here. Builder plumbing for `@blocking` routines.
dangerous routine blocking_call(entry: Addr, recipe: Addr)
    C::rf_blocking_call(entry: entry, arg: recipe)
    return

### The task block of a recipe (slot 0).
### :returns: the rf_task* as an Addr.
dangerous routine recipe_task(recipe: Addr) -> Addr
    return Hijacked[Addr](recipe).peek()

### Builds the Work for a `to[Work]` conversion: the recipe and the routine that runs it. Nothing runs until a verb.
### :returns: the Work recipe.
dangerous routine work_recipe[T](entry: Addr, recipe: Addr) -> Work[T]
    return Work[T](state: work_state[T](entry: entry, recipe: recipe), deadline: Duration(seconds: 0, nanoseconds: 0),
        has_deadline: false)

### Builds the work for a `to[Work]` conversion, not started: a fresh state cell over the recipe and the routine that
### runs it.
### :returns: the work.
dangerous routine work_state[T](entry: Addr, recipe: Addr) -> WorkState[T]
    var cell = rf_allocate_dynamic(size: recipe_slot_size() * 6)
    var words = Hijacked[U64](cell)
    words.poke_at_offset(at: recipe_slot_size() * 0, value: 0)
    words.poke_at_offset(at: recipe_slot_size() * 1, value: 0)
    words.poke_at_offset(at: recipe_slot_size() * 2, value: 0)
    words.poke_at_offset(at: recipe_slot_size() * 3, value: 0)
    words.poke_at_offset(at: recipe_slot_size() * 4, value: 1)
    words.poke_at_offset(at: recipe_slot_size() * 5, value: 0)
    return WorkState[T](cell: cell, entry: entry, recipe: recipe)

### Completes a recipe's task with what its work came to (the value, or the error it failed with) and frees the
### recipe. The work's failure stays in its Work: it surfaces where the Work is retrieved. The outcome MOVES into
### the task's box: its bytes are copied, then its tag is cleared, so the caller's teardown of `outcome` frees
### nothing the box now holds (a caught error is one object, freed once). Builder plumbing for the routine that runs
### a recipe.
dangerous routine work_finish[T](recipe: Addr, outcome: Hijacked[Check[T]])
    var task = recipe_task(recipe: recipe)
    C::rf_invalidate(ptr: recipe)
    var size = Check[T].data_size()
    var cell = rf_allocate_dynamic(size: size)
    rf_copy_bytes_at(dest: cell, src: outcome.address(), size: size)
    Hijacked[U64](outcome.address()).poke(value: 0)
    C::rf_task_complete_value(task: task, result: cell)
    return

### Reads word `slot` of the shared cell. A slot that holds a handle holds its address as a number.
### :returns: the word.
dangerous routine WorkState[T].cell_word(slot: U64) -> U64
    return Hijacked[U64](me.cell).peek_at_offset(offset: recipe_slot_size() * slot)

### Writes word `slot` of the shared cell.
dangerous routine WorkState[T].set_cell_word(slot: U64, value: U64)
    Hijacked[U64](me.cell).poke_at_offset(at: recipe_slot_size() * slot, value: value)
    return

### Where the work runs, or WAITING before a verb starts it.
### :returns: the place.
routine WorkState[T].place() -> WorkPlace
    danger
        var code = me.cell_word(slot: 0)
        if code == 1
            return WorkPlace.NOW
        if code == 2
            return WorkPlace.HERE
        if code == 3
            return WorkPlace.APART
        if code == 4
            return WorkPlace.DONE
    return WorkPlace.WAITING

### Records where the work runs.
dangerous routine WorkState[T].set_place(place: WorkPlace)
    var code: U64 = 0
    if place == WorkPlace.NOW
        code = 1
    elseif place == WorkPlace.HERE
        code = 2
    elseif place == WorkPlace.APART
        code = 3
    elseif place == WorkPlace.DONE
        code = 4
    me.set_cell_word(slot: 0, value: code)
    return

### The fiber handle (rf_coro*) for NOW and HERE, none before a verb starts the work.
### :returns: the fiber.
routine WorkState[T].coro() -> Addr
    danger
        return me.cell_word(slot: 1).to[Addr]()

### The task control block that holds the produced value, zero before a verb starts the work.
### :returns: the task.
routine WorkState[T].task() -> Addr
    danger
        return me.cell_word(slot: 2).to[Addr]()

### Starts the work at `place` if no verb started it yet: makes the task block (into recipe slot 0) and the fiber or
### thread that runs the recipe. Idempotent, so `retrieve`/`gather`/`race` call it on every Work they drive.
### Takes `me` by reference: the place and handles it sets are what every later verb reads.
### A `held` start makes the task block and the fiber or thread but leaves recipe slot 0 empty: the routine that runs
### the recipe waits there (`work_wait_released`) until `release()` writes the task, so the Works of a list are all
### ready before any of them runs.
### :returns: false if the work could not be started, or `place` is HERE inside a `start_now()` job.
dangerous routine WorkState[T].start_at(place: WorkPlace, held: Bool) -> Bool
    if me.place() != WorkPlace.WAITING or me.cancelled()
        return true
    if held
        me.set_cell_word(slot: 5, value: 1)
    if place == WorkPlace.APART
        me.set_cell_word(slot: 2, value: C::rf_task_create(kind: 1).to[U64]())
        if not held
            Hijacked[Addr](me.recipe).poke(value: me.task())
        me.set_place(place: WorkPlace.APART)
        if me.cell_word(slot: 3) != 0
            C::rf_task_request_cancel(task: me.task())
        return C::rf_task_spawn_threaded(task: me.task(), entry: me.entry, userdata: me.recipe) != 0
    var here = Addr.hollow()
    if place == WorkPlace.HERE
        here = C::rf_sched_here()
        if here.is_hollow()
            return false
    me.set_cell_word(slot: 2, value: C::rf_task_create(kind: 0).to[U64]())
    if not held
        Hijacked[Addr](me.recipe).poke(value: me.task())
    var coro = C::rf_coro_create(entry: me.entry, userdata: me.recipe, stack_size: 0)
    me.set_cell_word(slot: 1, value: coro.to[U64]())
    me.set_place(place: place)
    if me.cell_word(slot: 3) != 0
        C::rf_coro_request_cancel(coro: coro)
    if place == WorkPlace.HERE
        C::rf_sched_spawn(sched: here, coro: me.coro())
    else
        C::rf_sched_spawn_default(coro: me.coro())
    return true

### Starts the work if no verb started it yet, the way `retrieve` starts it: with `start_now()`. Builder and
### library plumbing for the verbs over a `List[Work[T]]`.
### :returns: false if the work could not be started.
dangerous routine WorkState[T].launch() -> Bool
    return me.start_at(place: WorkPlace.NOW, held: false)

### Starts the work with `start_now()` if no verb started it yet, held back until `release()` lets it go. Plumbing for
### the verbs over a list of jobs.
### :returns: false if the work could not be started.
dangerous routine WorkState[T].launch_held() -> Bool
    return me.start_at(place: WorkPlace.NOW, held: true)

### Whether no verb started the work yet.
### :returns: `true` while the work waits for a verb.
routine WorkState[T].is_waiting() -> Bool
    return me.place() == WorkPlace.WAITING

### Lets a work started held (`start_at(place:, held: true)`) go: writes its task into recipe slot 0, where the routine
### that runs the recipe waits for it. Does nothing for a work that is not held, so it can be called on every Work of
### a list.
dangerous routine WorkState[T].release()
    if me.cell_word(slot: 5) == 0
        return
    me.set_cell_word(slot: 5, value: 0)
    LLVM::atomic_store[U64](ptr: Hijacked[U64](me.recipe), value: me.cell_word(slot: 2))
    return

### Waits, at the start of the routine that runs a Work, until the verb that started it lets it go: a list's
### `start_now()` / `start_here()` / `start_apart()` makes every Work ready first and then lets them all go, so none runs
### before the others are ready. A Work started on its own is let go at once. Builder plumbing for the routine that
### runs a recipe.
dangerous routine work_wait_released(recipe: Addr)
    until LLVM::atomic_load[U64](ptr: Hijacked[U64](recipe)) != 0
        if C::rf_in_coroutine() != 0
            C::rf_coro_yield()
        else
            C::rf_waitfor_duration(secs: 0, ns: 1000)
    return

### Starts a verb's own place, or crashes with what went wrong: the Work was started already, or `start_here()`
### runs inside a `start_now()` job.
### :throws: {WorkStartError} if the Work was started already or cannot start at `place`.
### :throws: {TaskSpawnError} if the work could not be started.
failable routine WorkState[T].start_with(place: WorkPlace, verb: Text)
    if me.place() != WorkPlace.WAITING
        throw WorkStartError(reason: f"You called {verb} on a Work that was already started. Start a Work once.")
    danger
        if me.start_at(place: place, held: false)
            return
    if place == WorkPlace.HERE and me.place() == WorkPlace.WAITING
        throw WorkStartError(reason: "You called start_here() inside a job started with start_now(), which can move " +
            "between threads, so it has no thread of its own to run a job beside. Start it with start_now(), or " +
            "start the outer job with start_here() or start_apart().")
    throw TaskSpawnError()

### Starts the work now on the runtime's pool of threads, where it may run beside its siblings on any thread.
### Collect the result later with `retrieve()` or `gather()`.
### :throws: {WorkStartError} if the Work was started already.
### :throws: {TaskSpawnError} if the work could not be started.
failable routine WorkState[T].start_now()
    me.start_with(place: WorkPlace.NOW, verb: "start_now()")
    return

### Starts the work now on this thread, beside this thread's other `start_here()` jobs. They take turns only where
### one waits (`retrieve`, `waitfor`, a channel), so they can share values without a lock, and a job that blocks
### the thread stops them all. Collect the result later with `retrieve()` or `gather()`.
### :throws: {WorkStartError} if the Work was started already, or this runs inside a `start_now()` job.
### :throws: {TaskSpawnError} if the work could not be started.
failable routine WorkState[T].start_here()
    me.start_with(place: WorkPlace.HERE, verb: "start_here()")
    return

### Starts the work now on an OS thread of its own, where it may block or compute for long without stopping
### anything else. Collect the result later with `retrieve()` or `gather()`.
### :throws: {WorkStartError} if the Work was started already.
### :throws: {TaskSpawnError} if the work could not be started.
failable routine WorkState[T].start_apart()
    me.start_with(place: WorkPlace.APART, verb: "start_apart()")
    return

### Whether this Work runs as a fiber (NOW or HERE). Internal plumbing for `race!`, which dispatches on the
### place while reading List elements as temporaries (a direct field read on a generic List element miscompiles
### today, going through a member routine monomorphizes the receiver cleanly).
### :returns: true if it runs as a fiber, false if on a thread of its own.
routine WorkState[T].is_coro() -> Bool
    return me.place() != WorkPlace.APART

### The raw fiber handle (valid for NOW and HERE). Internal plumbing for `race!`.
### :returns: the rf_coro* as an Addr.
routine WorkState[T].coro_handle() -> Addr
    return me.coro()

### The raw task/result control block (fibers and threads both box their produced value here). Internal `race!`
### plumbing; also where the winner's value is read from.
### :returns: the rf_task* as an Addr.
routine WorkState[T].task_handle() -> Addr
    return me.task()

### Whether the work has finished (its value is ready to `retrieve!` without waiting). A racy snapshot
### for polling/heuristics, not a synchronization decision — it can flip to true the instant after you
### read it. A fiber is done at status COMPLETED (3) or CANCELLED (4, stopped at a wait on the cancel request), a
### thread at COMPLETED (4), and a Work no verb started is done only once it is cancelled.
### :returns: `true` if the work has completed.
routine WorkState[T].is_done() -> Bool
    # Work nothing started is done once it is cancelled: it never runs.
    if me.place() == WorkPlace.WAITING
        return me.cancelled()
    if me.place() == WorkPlace.DONE
        return true
    if me.place() != WorkPlace.APART
        if me.coro().is_hollow()
            return true
        danger
            var status = C::rf_coro_status_get(coro: me.coro())
            return status == 3 or status == 4
    danger
        return C::rf_task_status_get(task: me.task()) == 4

### Whether the work was started and has not yet finished. Racy snapshot (see `is_done`).
### :returns: `true` if still in flight.
routine WorkState[T].is_running() -> Bool
    return me.place() != WorkPlace.WAITING and not me.is_done()

### Requests cooperative cancellation of this job: sets a flag in NATIVE memory (reached through the handle)
### and returns. It never frees or unwinds. A fiber's next wait (`waitfor`, a channel) returns early, and a wait
### after that stops it, where its Work abandons it. A thread of its own sees it only through
### `cancellation_requested()` (a thread cannot be killed), and each of its waits returns early. A Work no verb started yet records the request in its
### cell, and sees it as soon as it starts.
dangerous routine WorkState[T].request_cancel()
    if me.place() == WorkPlace.DONE
        return
    me.set_cell_word(slot: 3, value: 1)
    if me.place() == WorkPlace.WAITING
        return
    if me.place() != WorkPlace.APART
        if not me.coro().is_hollow()
            C::rf_coro_request_cancel(coro: me.coro())
        return
    C::rf_task_request_cancel(task: me.task())
    return

### Waits for cancelled work to stop and frees what ran it, dropping what it came to. Work nothing started is
### only marked done. Plumbing for `cancel_all` and for the teardown of a Work nobody retrieved.
### :param reports_failure: Whether a failure the work came to (other than stopping on the cancel request) crashes
###     here instead of being dropped: true for a Work dropped without `cancel()`, whose failure nothing else would
###     see.
dangerous routine WorkState[T].wind_down(reports_failure: Bool)
    if me.place() == WorkPlace.DONE
        return
    if me.place() == WorkPlace.WAITING
        me.set_place(place: WorkPlace.DONE)
        return
    if me.place() != WorkPlace.APART
        C::rf_sched_run_until_default(coro: me.coro())
        # A fiber that stopped at a wait came to nothing: reaping abandons it.
        if me.stopped()
            me.reap()
            return
    elseif C::rf_in_coroutine() != 0
        until C::rf_task_await_coro(task: me.task()) != 0
            C::rf_sched_park_external()
    else
        drop C::rf_task_wait(task: me.task())
    # The outcome is dropped: its scope's teardown frees it. A failure of a Work nobody retrieved or cancelled is
    # not lost: the bare call to the failable `crash_unretrieved` crashes with it.
    var outcome = me.take_outcome()
    me.reap()
    if reports_failure
        me.crash_unretrieved(outcome: outcome)
    return

### Throws the failure in `outcome`, if it holds one other than {CancelRequested}. Called bare where a Work nobody
### retrieved is dropped, so its failure crashes the program with the failure's own message.
### :param outcome: What the work came to.
### :throws: The failure the work came to.
@readonly
failable routine WorkState[T].crash_unretrieved(outcome: Check[T])
    when outcome
        is CancelRequested =>
            return
        is Crashables as error =>
            throw error
        is T =>
            return

### Moves the finished work's outcome out of its task block and frees the block.
### :returns: what the work came to.
dangerous routine WorkState[T].take_outcome() -> Check[T]
    var payload = C::rf_task_result_payload(task: me.task())
    var outcome = Hijacked[Check[T]](from: payload).peek()
    C::rf_invalidate(ptr: payload)
    return outcome

### Takes the outcome of finished work and frees what ran it, then gives its value: its error is rethrown here,
### where the Work is retrieved, and a Work that was cancelled reports that instead of its value. The outcome is
### this routine's own, so a rethrown error moves out of it.
### :throws: {CancelRequested} if the Work was cancelled.
### :returns: The value the work returned.
failable routine WorkState[T].settle() -> T
    danger
        # A fiber that stopped at a wait on the cancel request came to nothing: reaping abandons it.
        if me.stopped()
            me.reap()
            throw CancelRequested()
        var outcome = me.take_outcome()
        me.reap()
        if me.cancelled()
            throw CancelRequested()
        when outcome
            is T as value =>
                return value
            is Crashables as error =>
                throw error

### Whether the work is a fiber that stopped at a wait on the cancel request: it is over, and came to nothing.
### :returns: `true` once it stopped that way.
dangerous routine WorkState[T].stopped() -> Bool
    if me.place() == WorkPlace.APART or me.coro().is_hollow()
        return false
    return C::rf_coro_status_get(coro: me.coro()) == 4  # RF_CORO_CANCELLED

### Whether `cancel` was called on this Work.
### :returns: `true` once cancellation was requested.
routine WorkState[T].cancelled() -> Bool
    danger
        return me.cell_word(slot: 3) != 0

### Asks the work to stop. Work nothing started yet never runs. Running work sees the request at its next wait
### (`waitfor`, `retrieve`, a channel) or through `cancellation_requested()`, and stops there. Retrieving a cancelled
### Work waits for it to stop, then throws {CancelRequested}.
routine WorkState[T].cancel()
    danger
        me.request_cancel()
    return

### Waits for the work to finish and returns its produced value. A Work no verb started is started with
### `start_now()` first. UNCOLORED: inside a fiber it PARKS (siblings run meanwhile) instead of blocking, on a
### plain thread it blocks, and waiting for a `start_here()` job on its own thread runs that thread's jobs until
### this one finishes. A deadline (`job.waitfor(d).retrieve()`) races completion against the timer and throws
### {TaskTimeoutError} if the deadline wins: it needs a `start_apart()` job, and a Work no verb started yet is
### started that way. `retrieve!` reads the value out and reaps what
### ran: a thread's task goes to the worker↔consumer rendezvous, so on a deadline give-up the still-running
### thread frees its own block.
### :throws: {WorkStartError} if the value was taken already, or a deadline was set on a fiber.
### :throws: {CancelRequested} if the Work was cancelled.
### :throws: Whatever the work threw: its failure surfaces here, not where it ran.
### :throws: {TaskSpawnError} if the work could not be started.
### :throws: {TaskTimeoutError} if a deadline was set and elapsed before completion.
### :returns: The value the routine returned.
failable routine WorkState[T].retrieve(deadline: Duration, has_deadline: Bool) -> T
    if me.place() == WorkPlace.DONE
        throw WorkStartError(reason: "You called retrieve() on a Work whose value was already taken. " +
            "Retrieve a Work once.")
    # Cancelled before anything started it: it never runs.
    if me.place() == WorkPlace.WAITING and me.cancelled()
        danger
            me.set_place(place: WorkPlace.DONE)
        throw CancelRequested()
    # A deadline gives up on work that keeps running, which only a thread of its own can do: a Work nothing
    # started yet starts there.
    if has_deadline and (me.place() == WorkPlace.NOW or me.place() == WorkPlace.HERE)
        throw WorkStartError(reason: "You gave retrieve() a deadline, but this Work was started to take turns " +
            "with other work, and work like that can't be left behind while it runs. Give the deadline before " +
            "anything starts the Work: one nothing started yet starts where it can be left behind.")
    danger
        var started = false
        if has_deadline
            started = me.start_at(place: WorkPlace.APART, held: false)
        else
            started = me.launch()
        if not started
            throw TaskSpawnError()
    if me.place() != WorkPlace.APART
        danger
            C::rf_sched_run_until_default(coro: me.coro())
            # The outcome is taken, then the fiber and its result block are reaped: this is the single consumer.
            return me.settle()

    danger
        if has_deadline
            var total = deadline.total_nanoseconds()
            if C::rf_in_coroutine() != 0
                # Inside a scheduler-driven coroutine: race the worker against the deadline WITHOUT
                # blocking. Park on a deadline timer that is also externally wakeable; siblings run.
                # 0 => the deadline elapsed first (timeout); 1 => completed. Do NOT free here — destroy
                # joins the still-running worker before freeing (the timeout does not stop it).
                var outcome = C::rf_task_await_coro_deadline(task: me.task(), timeout_ns: total.to[U64]())
                if outcome == 0
                    # Deadline won; give up. Reap hands the free to the still-running worker, which frees its own
                    # block as its final act (non-blocking; destroy is a no-op).
                    me.reap()
                    throw TaskTimeoutError()
            else
                var secs = total // 1000000000
                var nanos = (total - secs * 1000000000).to[U32]()
                var kind = C::rf_task_wait_within(task: me.task(), secs: secs, ns: nanos)
                if kind != 1
                    # Deadline won on a plain thread; give up. Reap lets the still-running worker free its own
                    # block on exit (non-blocking; destroy is a no-op).
                    me.reap()
                    throw TaskTimeoutError()
        else
            if C::rf_in_coroutine() != 0
                # Inside a scheduler-driven coroutine: park instead of blocking. Register as the task's
                # awaiter and yield; the worker wakes us (cross-thread) on completion. The loop re-checks
                # under the lock so no wake is lost, and tolerates spurious wakes.
                until C::rf_task_await_coro(task: me.task()) != 0
                    C::rf_sched_park_external()
            else
                drop C::rf_task_wait(task: me.task())

        # The outcome is taken, then reaped: this is the single consumer, so the worker is freed exactly once.
        return me.settle()

### Reaps this job's work — the SOLE freeing path, called EXACTLY ONCE by the consumer that is done
### with it (`retrieve!` after reading the value or a deadline give-up; `race!` over its whole set).
### `destruct` reaps only a Work no verb consumed (through `wind_down`), so nothing is freed twice. For a
### coroutine: a completed one
### is disposed + deleted, an in-flight one is disposed + abandoned (its cancellation shadow stack runs),
### and its result block is freed. For a thread: `rf_task_release` hands the free to the worker↔consumer
### rendezvous — a still-running worker (a deadline give-up, or a race loser) frees its own block as its
### final act, so reaping is NON-BLOCKING (no join).
dangerous routine WorkState[T].reap()
    if me.place() == WorkPlace.WAITING or me.place() == WorkPlace.DONE
        return
    if me.place() != WorkPlace.APART
        if not me.coro().is_hollow()
            var status = C::rf_coro_status_get(coro: me.coro())
            # Dispose race-free against the work-stealing workers. `rf_sched_dispose_default` detaches the
            # coroutine from the pool and, under the scheduler lock, decides whether WE may free it now (returns
            # 1) or the free was DEFERRED (returns 0) because a worker still holds it (RUNNING, or mid-pop between
            # the deque pop and the RUNNING store). Freeing a coroutine a worker is one step from resuming is the
            # M:N use-after-free (worker co_switch()es a freed stack → jump to NULL → SIGSEGV); deferring lets the
            # worker free it from its completion path instead. A plain `unschedule + free` cannot see the mid-pop
            # window and races that resume.
            if status == 3  # RF_CORO_COMPLETED
                if C::rf_sched_dispose_default(coro: me.coro()) == 1
                    C::rf_coro_delete(coro: me.coro())
            else
                # Not driven to completion (a race loser). If disposal succeeds now (parked / idle / removed) run
                # its cancellation shadow stack + free (abandon); if deferred (still RUNNING) the coroutine runs to
                # completion and the worker frees it — its own scope teardown replaces the abandon walk.
                if C::rf_sched_dispose_default(coro: me.coro()) == 1
                    C::rf_coro_abandon(coro: me.coro())
                    # The routine that runs the recipe frees it as it finishes, which an abandoned fiber never
                    # does.
                    C::rf_invalidate(ptr: me.recipe)
            C::rf_task_destroy(task: me.task())
        # The fiber and task are freed: the cell forgets them, so a query on this Work after it was consumed
        # (`is_done()` / `is_running()`) reports "done" instead of reading freed memory.
        me.set_cell_word(slot: 1, value: 0)
        me.set_cell_word(slot: 2, value: 0)
        me.set_cell_word(slot: 0, value: 4)
        return

    # Threaded: hand the free to the worker↔consumer rendezvous. A completed worker's block is freed here
    # or at the worker's exit (whichever is last); a still-running worker (deadline give-up / race loser)
    # frees its own block on exit — no join.
    C::rf_task_release(task: me.task())
    me.set_cell_word(slot: 2, value: 0)
    me.set_cell_word(slot: 0, value: 4)
    return

### Takes one more hold on the work, for one more Work over it (Suflae's `waitfor` makes one).
### :returns: The same work.
dangerous routine WorkState[T].add_holder() -> WorkState[T]
    me.set_cell_word(slot: 4, value: me.cell_word(slot: 4) +% 1)
    return me

### Lets go of one hold on the work. The last holder ends it: work that still runs is cancelled and waited for
### (what it came to is dropped), work nothing started never runs, and the state cell is freed. Work a verb consumed
### only frees its cell. Each owning Work's teardown calls it once.
dangerous routine WorkState[T].dispose()
    var holders = me.cell_word(slot: 4) -% 1
    if holders != 0
        me.set_cell_word(slot: 4, value: holders)
        return
    if me.place() != WorkPlace.DONE
        # Work the program cancelled itself drops its outcome. Otherwise nobody took what it came to, and a failure
        # in it crashes here instead of vanishing.
        var reports_failure = not me.cancelled()
        me.request_cancel()
        me.wind_down(reports_failure: reports_failure)
    C::rf_invalidate(ptr: me.cell)
    return

### Starts the work now on the runtime's pool of threads, where it may run beside its siblings on any thread.
### Collect the result later with `retrieve()` or `gather()`.
### :throws: {WorkStartError} if the Work was started already.
### :throws: {TaskSpawnError} if the work could not be started.
failable routine Work[T].start_now()
    me.state.start_now()
    return

### Starts the work now on this thread, beside this thread's other `start_here()` jobs. They take turns only where
### one waits (`retrieve`, `waitfor`, a channel), so they can share values without a lock, and a job that blocks
### the thread stops them all. Collect the result later with `retrieve()` or `gather()`.
### :throws: {WorkStartError} if the Work was started already, or this runs inside a `start_now()` job.
### :throws: {TaskSpawnError} if the work could not be started.
failable routine Work[T].start_here()
    me.state.start_here()
    return

### Starts the work now on an OS thread of its own, where it may block or compute for long without stopping
### anything else. Collect the result later with `retrieve()` or `gather()`.
### :throws: {WorkStartError} if the Work was started already.
### :throws: {TaskSpawnError} if the work could not be started.
failable routine Work[T].start_apart()
    me.state.start_apart()
    return

### Attaches a deadline to this Work's next `retrieve!`, for `job.waitfor(5s).retrieve()` on a Work started with
### `start_apart()` (or that nothing started yet, which then starts that way).
### :param deadline: The maximum time `retrieve!` will wait.
### :returns: This Work, carrying the deadline.
routine Work[T].waitfor(deadline: Duration) -> Modifying[Work[T]]
    me.deadline = deadline
    me.has_deadline = true
    return me.modify()

### Waits for the work to finish and returns its produced value. A Work no verb started is started with
### `start_now()` first. Inside a fiber it parks (siblings run meanwhile), on a plain thread it blocks. A deadline
### from `waitfor` races completion against the timer and throws {TaskTimeoutError} if the deadline wins.
### :throws: {WorkStartError} if the value was taken already, or a deadline was set on a fiber.
### :throws: {CancelRequested} if the Work was cancelled.
### :throws: Whatever the work threw: its failure surfaces here, not where it ran.
### :throws: {TaskSpawnError} if the work could not be started.
### :throws: {TaskTimeoutError} if a deadline was set and elapsed before completion.
### :returns: The value the routine returned.
failable routine Work[T].retrieve() -> T
    # A deadline from `waitfor` is for this one retrieve.
    var has_deadline = me.has_deadline
    me.has_deadline = false
    return me.state.retrieve(deadline: me.deadline, has_deadline: has_deadline)

### Whether the work has finished (its value is ready to `retrieve!` without waiting). A racy snapshot for
### polling, not a synchronization decision.
### :returns: `true` if the work has completed.
routine Work[T].is_done() -> Bool
    return me.state.is_done()

### Whether the work was started and has not yet finished. Racy snapshot (see `is_done`).
### :returns: `true` if still in flight.
routine Work[T].is_running() -> Bool
    return me.state.is_running()

### Asks the work to stop. Work nothing started yet never runs. Running work sees the request at its next wait
### (`waitfor`, `retrieve`, a channel) or through `cancellation_requested()`, and stops there. Retrieving a cancelled
### Work waits for it to stop, then throws {CancelRequested}.
routine Work[T].cancel()
    me.state.cancel()
    return

### Whether `cancel` was called on this Work.
### :returns: `true` once cancellation was requested.
routine Work[T].cancelled() -> Bool
    return me.state.cancelled()

### Ends a Work the place that keeps it drops (`drop job`, or the end of the scope that holds it): work that still runs is
### cancelled and waited for, work nothing started never runs.
@override
routine Work[T].destruct()
    danger
        me.state.dispose()
        return

# --- Structured concurrency over a set of jobs -----------------------------------------------
# A `List[Work[T]]` bound to a name IS the structured-concurrency scope: the list keeping its Works already
# guarantees no child outlives it (the list's teardown cancels and waits for every Work still running). So the
# scope operations are MEMBER routines on that list — `race!` / `gather!` / `cancel_all` — read as
# `jobs.gather()`. They work on the list in place (`me` is by-reference): they never consume or free it, so
# nothing is handed over behind the call, and cleanup stays with the list's own teardown at scope exit.
# Each Work is worked on in place (`me[i]`), so nothing double-frees.

### Starts every Work of the list now on the runtime's pool of threads, all together: each one is made ready first
### and then they are let go at once, so none runs before the others are ready. Collect the results later with
### `gather()` (or `race()`, `next_finished()`, `gather_all()`).
### :throws: {WorkStartError} if a Work of the list was started already.
### :throws: {TaskSpawnError} if a Work could not be started.
failable routine List[Work[T]].start_now()
    me.start_together(place: WorkPlace.NOW, verb: "start_now()")
    return

### Starts every Work of the list now on this thread, beside its other `start_here()` jobs, all together: each one is
### made ready first and then they are let go at once. They take turns only where one waits (`retrieve`, `waitfor`, a
### channel). Collect the results later with `gather()`.
### :throws: {WorkStartError} if a Work of the list was started already, or this runs inside a `start_now()` job.
### :throws: {TaskSpawnError} if a Work could not be started.
failable routine List[Work[T]].start_here()
    me.start_together(place: WorkPlace.HERE, verb: "start_here()")
    return

### Starts every Work of the list now, each on an OS thread of its own, all together: every thread is made first and
### then they are let go at once, so none runs before the others are ready. Collect the results later with `gather()`.
### :throws: {WorkStartError} if a Work of the list was started already.
### :throws: {TaskSpawnError} if a Work could not be started.
failable routine List[Work[T]].start_apart()
    me.start_together(place: WorkPlace.APART, verb: "start_apart()")
    return

### Starts every Work of the list at `place`, held, then lets them all go. Plumbing for the list's `start_now()`,
### `start_here()` and `start_apart()`.
### :throws: {WorkStartError} if a Work of the list was started already, or HERE runs inside a `start_now()` job.
### :throws: {TaskSpawnError} if a Work could not be started.
failable routine List[Work[T]].start_together(place: WorkPlace, verb: Text)
    var n = me.count()
    var i: U64 = 0
    until i >= n
        if me[i].state.place() != WorkPlace.WAITING
            throw WorkStartError(reason: f"You called {verb} on a list whose Work at position {i} was already " +
                "started. Start a Work once.")
        i = i + 1
    danger
        i = 0
        until i >= n
            if not me[i].state.start_at(place: place, held: true)
                me.release_all()
                if place == WorkPlace.HERE
                    throw WorkStartError(reason: "You called start_here() inside a job started with start_now(), " +
                        "which can move between threads, so it has no thread of its own to run jobs beside. Start " +
                        "them with start_now(), or start the outer job with start_here() or start_apart().")
                throw TaskSpawnError()
            i = i + 1
        me.release_all()
    return

### Starts every Work of the list that nothing started yet with `start_now()`, all together, the way `gather` and
### `race` start them. Plumbing for the verbs over a list.
### :returns: false if a Work could not be started.
dangerous routine List[Work[T]].launch_together() -> Bool
    var n = me.count()
    var started = true
    var i: U64 = 0
    until i >= n
        if not me[i].state.start_at(place: WorkPlace.NOW, held: true)
            started = false
        i = i + 1
    me.release_all()
    return started

### Lets every held Work of the list go.
dangerous routine List[Work[T]].release_all()
    var n = me.count()
    var i: U64 = 0
    until i >= n
        me[i].state.release()
        i = i + 1
    return

### Races the jobs, driving them CONCURRENTLY until the FIRST one finishes, and returns that
### winner's value. Works on the list in place — call it `jobs.race()`. A coroutine loser is disposed/abandoned here, a
### threaded loser's block is freed through the worker↔consumer rendezvous (its worker frees it on exit).
### Mixed coroutine/thread sets are supported.
### :throws: {WorkStartError} if the list is empty (there is no work to race).
### :throws: {TaskSpawnError} if a Work could not be started.
### :returns: The value produced by the first Work to finish.
failable routine List[Work[T]].race() -> T
    danger
        var n = me.count()
        if n == 0
            throw WorkStartError(reason: "You called race() on an empty list, so there is no work to race. " +
                "Put the Works in the list first.")

        # Start the whole set together so it runs CONCURRENTLY, then race.
        if not me.launch_together()
            throw TaskSpawnError()
        var race = C::rf_race_begin()
        var i: U64 = 0
        until i >= n
            if me[i].state.is_coro()
                C::rf_race_add_coro(race: race, coro: me[i].state.coro_handle())
            else
                C::rf_race_add_task(race: race, task: me[i].state.task_handle())
            i = i + 1

        # Drive until the first competitor completes; `winner` indexes the List in add order. Fibers and threads
        # box their produced value into a task block referenced by the Work, so extraction is uniform.
        var winner = C::rf_race_wait(race: race)
        C::rf_race_end(race: race)
        var w = winner.to[U64]()
        # race! is the sole consumer of this set: the losers are never retrieved. Reap every loser exactly once
        # here (a still-running loser's worker frees its own block on exit, non-blocking), then settle the
        # winner, which reaps it. The List's own teardown then only frees each Work's cell.
        var k: U64 = 0
        until k >= n
            if k != w
                me[k].state.reap()
            k = k + 1
        return me[w].state.settle()

### Drives the jobs CONCURRENTLY and waits for ALL of them to finish, returning their results in
### INPUT order. The twin of `race!` (first finisher); `gather!` returns every finisher. Works on the
### list in place — call it `jobs.gather()`. Each Work is driven with the same uncolored `retrieve!` logic,
### so coroutine children progress on this thread's scheduler and thread children run on their OS
### threads; total wait is the SLOWEST child, not the sum. FAIL-FAST: if any Work's routine throws,
### that throw propagates out and the remaining children are cancelled and waited for when the list tears
### down at scope exit. An empty set yields an empty list.
### :returns: The values produced by every Work, in the order they appear in the list.
failable routine List[Work[T]].gather() -> List[T]
    danger
        var n = me.count()
        # Start the WHOLE set FIRST, together, so the children run CONCURRENTLY, then retrieve each. Driving
        # one-by-one would serialize the children into N× the wall-clock. Starting is idempotent, so
        # retrieve!'s own start below is a no-op on these already-started recipes.
        if not me.launch_together()
            throw TaskSpawnError()
        var results = List[T]()
        var i: U64 = 0
        until i >= n
            var value = me[i].retrieve()
            results.add_last(value: value)
            i = i + 1
        return results

### Cancels every Work in the set and waits for each to stop, dropping what it came to. Work nothing started yet
### never runs. Works on the list in place — call it `jobs.cancel_all()`. Running work sees the request at its next wait
### (`waitfor`, `retrieve`, a channel) or through `cancellation_requested()`, and stops there: a thread of its own
### cannot be stopped from outside.
routine List[Work[T]].cancel_all()
    danger
        var n = me.count()
        var i: U64 = 0
        until i >= n
            me[i].state.request_cancel()
            i = i + 1
        i = 0
        until i >= n
            me[i].state.wind_down(reports_failure: false)
            i = i + 1
    return

### Waits until one of the jobs finishes, takes it out of the list and returns it, starting the ones nothing
### started. Called again and again it hands the jobs over in the order they finish, so each result is handled as
### soon as it is there. `retrieve` the returned Work for its value or its failure.
### :throws: {WorkStartError} if the list is empty (there is no work to wait for).
### :throws: {TaskSpawnError} if a Work cannot be started.
### :returns: The first Work of the list to finish.
failable routine List[Work[T]].next_finished() -> Work[T]
    danger
        var n = me.count()
        if n == 0
            throw WorkStartError(reason: "You called next_finished() on an empty list, so there is no work to wait " +
                "for. Put the Works in the list first.")
        if not me.launch_together()
            throw TaskSpawnError()
        var race = C::rf_race_begin()
        var i: U64 = 0
        until i >= n
            if me[i].state.is_coro()
                C::rf_race_add_coro(race: race, coro: me[i].state.coro_handle())
            else
                C::rf_race_add_task(race: race, task: me[i].state.task_handle())
            i = i + 1
        var winner = C::rf_race_wait(race: race)
        C::rf_race_end(race: race)
        return me.remove_at(index: winner.to[U64]())

### Drives the jobs concurrently and waits for all of them, returning each one's result or failure in INPUT
### order: a Work that fails does not stop the others or hide their results (`gather` throws the first failure).
### :returns: Each Work's value or failure, in the order they appear in the list.
routine List[Work[T]].gather_all() -> List[Check[T]]
    danger
        drop me.launch_together()
        var results = List[Check[T]]()
        for job in me
            results.add_last(value: check job.retrieve())
        return results

### Takes the finished jobs out of the list and returns them, in order, leaving the ones still waiting or
### running in place. A long-running server collects the work of its departed clients this way: rebuilding the list
### with `where` would drop the old list, and with it the work that still runs. `retrieve` what a taken Work came to,
### or `drop` it.
### :returns: The finished jobs.
routine List[Work[T]].take_finished() -> List[Work[T]]
    var finished = List[Work[T]]()
    var i: U64 = 0
    until i >= me.count()
        if me[i].is_done()
            finished.add_last(value: me.remove_at(index: i))
        else
            i = i + 1
    return finished

### Waits for `duration` to elapse. UNCOLORED: inside a scheduler-driven coroutine it PARKS — the
### scheduler runs other coroutines meanwhile and resumes this one when the timer fires — so it
### costs no thread; on a plain thread it sleeps the thread. The same `waitfor(5s)` does the right
### thing in either context, which is why it is preferable to a raw `sleep`.
### :param duration: How long to wait.
routine waitfor(duration: Duration)
    # A job asked to stop is told by its first wait after the request, which returns early (a cancel also ends a
    # wait in progress), so its body can poll `cancellation_requested()` and return. A fiber that waits again stops
    # at that wait for good: its Work abandons it, tearing down what it built. A thread can't be stopped from
    # outside, so each of its waits returns early.
    danger
        if C::rf_coro_cancel_at_wait() != 0
            return
        var total = duration.total_nanoseconds()
        if C::rf_in_coroutine() != 0
            C::rf_sched_park_timer(delay_ns: total.to[U64]())
            drop C::rf_coro_cancel_at_wait()
        else
            var secs = total // 1000000000
            var nanos = (total - secs * 1000000000).to[U32]()
            C::rf_waitfor_duration(secs: secs, ns: nanos)
    return

### Cooperatively hands the current turn back to the scheduler, then immediately re-queues this
### coroutine as READY — the scheduler runs the other ready coroutines once before resuming here on
### the next tick. Unlike `waitfor`, it waits for NOTHING: it gives up the turn but stays runnable, so
### resumption is unconditional (no timer, no event). Use it to interleave a long yield-free stretch
### of CPU work with siblings; prefer `waitfor` or a channel op when there is an actual event to await.
### UNCOLORED: outside a scheduler-driven coroutine (a plain thread) there is no turn to yield, so it
### is a no-op.
routine yield_now()
    danger
        if C::rf_in_coroutine() != 0
            C::rf_coro_yield()
    return

### Whether cooperative cancellation has been requested for the currently-running job, a fiber or a thread
### of its own. A job body polls this and returns early to stop
### cooperatively; this is the ONLY way to halt a CPU-bound worker thread (a thread cannot be killed).
### A coroutine's `waitfor` also honors the request by returning early. Outside any job it is always
### false. :returns: true if cancellation was requested for this job.
routine cancellation_requested() -> Bool
    return C::rf_cancel_requested() != 0
